Create one high-fidelity visual concept for the Urkaaaz Android Shop screen.
This is a screen-design study only; do not edit production code, add shop
functionality, or generate a production background or runtime asset.

## Reference inputs

- The user will attach screenshots of the current Loadout screen, Campaign
  screen, and newly designed Dashboard. Treat all three as equal references
  for the shared Urkaaaz UI style: painterly fantasy scenery, dark translucent
  panels, carved wood/iron details, restrained warm-gold trim, pale readable
  text, and consistent framed buttons.
- Use `assets_src/shop/screen-concepts/shop_background_reference.png` as the
  exact background layer. The supplied seller is already part of that image;
  preserve his identity, clothing, colors, proportions, and placement. Do not
  repaint the background, replace the seller, clone him, or hide him behind
  UI panels.
- Use the screenshots for style, not as layouts to copy. The Shop must feel
  like the same game as the references while having its own market composition.
- The legacy Shop background at
  `~/workspace/orc-catapult/app/src/main/res/drawable-nodpi/shop_background.png`
  contains a different embedded shopkeeper and composition. Do not use it as
  the background or combine it with the saved reference image.
- The saved background is 1671 × 941. Scale it proportionally to the required
  1920 × 1080 canvas; only crop the negligible edge difference needed to fill
  the canvas. Never stretch or regenerate it.
- The legacy Shop information architecture is a useful content reference:
  SPELLS and AMMUNITION tabs, a gold balance panel, an item grid and an item
  details view. Do not copy its complete layout or use its outdated visual
  theme.
- Prefer existing approved game item names and icons where available. Do not
  invent new items, prices, ownership rules, stock counts, mechanics, or
  effects. If exact values are not provided as inputs, use neutral live-text
  placeholders such as `—`; do not bake changeable values into artwork.

## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG: `shop_screen_concept_01.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/shop/screen-concepts/`.
- Canvas: exactly 1920 × 1080 pixels, landscape 16:9.
- Show the complete Android screen edge-to-edge. No phone frame, device
  mockup, presentation board, annotations, alternate screens, watermark, or
  signature.
- This is a visual concept for review. Keep scenery, seller, panels, controls,
  icons, and text visually separable so the screen can be rebuilt with native
  Android views and individual assets. Do not flatten the interface into one
  production background.

## Shop content and hierarchy

- Make the seller clearly visible and recognizable as the shopkeeper. Position
  him on the left or center-left, naturally behind or beside a market counter,
  facing toward the offer panel. Keep his face and most of his silhouette
  unobstructed.
- Place a compact top header with a `BACK` control and a clear `SHOP` title.
  Back returns to the preceding Dashboard; do not add a direct match or
  Loadout route.
- Include a compact `ORCOINS` balance panel in the header or near the offer
  area. Keep the amount as live UI text, separate from the art. Do not invent
  a profile balance.
- Place the main catalog panel on the right or center-right, leaving enough
  space for the attached seller. Keep its silhouette and the main action
  readable at landscape mobile size.
- Provide exactly two top-level catalog tabs: `SPELLS` and `AMMUNITION`.
  Do not label ammunition as `ARTIFACTS`.
- Show a concise grid of existing catalog items with each item's icon, name,
  and a distinct live price/owned/stock area. Use item names and iconography
  from the game catalog when supplied; do not use deprecated `FIRE_FLASK` or
  `CLUSTER_BOMB` names.
- Show only the items for the selected tab. Do not mix spell cards and
  ammunition cards in one simultaneous grid.
- Show one selected item's details in a clearly separated panel: live item
  name, a short description/features area, price or owned/stock status, and a
  single visible `BUY`/stock action treatment. This is a proposed visual
  affordance only, not a claim that purchases currently work in Urkaaaz.
- Keep any selected-item treatment and category highlight restrained; reserve
  the brightest gold for the selected tab or primary item action, not every
  card.
- Include no RESET utility, language selector, dashboard profile summary, or
  unrelated navigation controls on this screen.

## Layout direction

- Use a full-bleed, painterly fantasy market/shop atmosphere that complements
  the three attached screen references. Maintain good contrast behind controls.
- Preserve the seller already visible in the exact background. Keep his face
  and defining accessories unobstructed by foreground UI.
- Use a strong left-to-right composition: seller and counter on the left,
  catalog and item details on the right. Keep all essential controls within
  generous screen-edge safe margins.
- Keep the offer area organized and scan-friendly: tabs above item cards,
  consistent card sizes, readable names, and a clearly selected item. Avoid
  dense decoration behind small text.
- Use the game's Cinzel SemiBold-style typography for headings and prominent
  labels, with compact, high-contrast supporting text.
- Keep item icons, prices, stock/ownership labels, descriptions, and buttons
  visually separable and suitable for live UI. Do not bake readable UI text
  into the scenery.

## Shared component treatment

- Match the shared appearance of Loadout, Campaign, and the new Dashboard:
  painterly scenery; charcoal/dark-iron panels; carved wood accents;
  restrained bronze/gold edging; and pale, high-contrast typography.
- Match their header/frame proportions, corner treatments, border thickness,
  button depth, selected/highlight gold, and level of ornament.
- Reuse existing approved UI button/frame/icon assets when available to the
  generation tool. If the required files are not accessible, keep the concept
  layers separable; do not draw replacements that conflict with attached
  references.
- Treat the complete background image as one fixed source layer and keep all
  shop panels, controls, item cards, and text as separate foreground UI layers.
  Do not add a second seller or a generated portrait in place of him.
- Maintain a clear distinction between catalog items and the selected-item
  detail area. Do not make every card equally prominent.

## Negative requirements

- No different visual theme from the attached Loadout, Campaign, and Dashboard
  references.
- No duplicate or hidden seller; no use of the different legacy Shop
  background.
- No copied Campaign map, chapter selector, level markers, route, or
  `START LEVEL` control.
- No copied Loadout carousels, spell/unit/ammunition selection panels, or
  `START MATCH` control.
- No invented items, prices, stocks, gold balances, rewards, ownership states,
  purchase rules, or gameplay effects.
- No claim that Shop purchases or inventory settlement are implemented in the
  current Urkaaaz app; this output is a design concept only.
- No advertising, reward-video, social, login, online, or account controls.
- No Dashboard RESET or language-change controls.
- No gibberish, pseudo-text, tiny unreadable labels, unrelated battle UI,
  clutter, device bezel, watermark, or signature.

## Review checklist

- Confirm the result is one complete 1920 × 1080 landscape Shop concept with
  no device frame.
- Confirm its visual language is coherent with all three attached screen
  references without copying their full layouts.
- Confirm the exact saved background is used without stretching or repainting,
  and that its embedded seller remains prominent, recognizable, and
  unobstructed.
- Confirm `SPELLS` and `AMMUNITION` are the two catalog tabs.
- Confirm the gold balance, prices, stock and item details are separate live
  UI elements, not baked into the background.
- Confirm the selected-item details and one proposed purchase action are clear
  without implying that shop mechanics are already implemented.
- Confirm the composition includes no fake item, price, balance, ownership
  status, or unimplemented feature.
- Use the result for visual review only; do not import it as a single runtime
  screen background.
